{"title":"field notes on my creative system","link":"https://keningzhu.com/journal/field-notes-on-my-creative-system","date":1748207760000,"content":" \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n    \n  \n    \n\n      \n\n      \n        <figure>\n          \n        \n        \n\n        \n          \n            \n          \n            \n                \n                \n                \n                \n                \n                \n                \n                <img src=\"https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/51e8148de4b01c1eb79c1977/dae03a7d-a458-4b2c-944d-6c9bd07b70b0/systemfieldnotes.gif?format=1000w\" width=\"1920\" height=\"1440\" srcset=\"https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/51e8148de4b01c1eb79c1977/dae03a7d-a458-4b2c-944d-6c9bd07b70b0/systemfieldnotes.gif?format=100w 100w, https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/51e8148de4b01c1eb79c1977/dae03a7d-a458-4b2c-944d-6c9bd07b70b0/systemfieldnotes.gif?format=300w 300w, https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/51e8148de4b01c1eb79c1977/dae03a7d-a458-4b2c-944d-6c9bd07b70b0/systemfieldnotes.gif?format=500w 500w, https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/51e8148de4b01c1eb79c1977/dae03a7d-a458-4b2c-944d-6c9bd07b70b0/systemfieldnotes.gif?format=750w 750w, https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/51e8148de4b01c1eb79c1977/dae03a7d-a458-4b2c-944d-6c9bd07b70b0/systemfieldnotes.gif?format=1000w 1000w, https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/51e8148de4b01c1eb79c1977/dae03a7d-a458-4b2c-944d-6c9bd07b70b0/systemfieldnotes.gif?format=1500w 1500w, https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/51e8148de4b01c1eb79c1977/dae03a7d-a458-4b2c-944d-6c9bd07b70b0/systemfieldnotes.gif?format=2500w 2500w\" loading=\"lazy\" />\n\n            \n          \n        \n          \n        \n\n        \n      \n        </figure>\n      \n\n    \n  \n\n\n  \n\n\n \n\n\n  <p>a tiny exploration of how my system works<br /><em>inspired by </em><a href=\"https://keningzhu.com/creative-systems\"><em>creative systems</em></a></p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n  \n  \n\n\n\n<hr />\n\n\n  <p>I made my first version of this <strong>systems field guide</strong> while teaching <a href=\"https://keningzhu.com/creative-systems\">creative systems</a> — as a way to capture what I know about my specific creative body — what it values, desires, and needs to feel in flow. </p><p>I previously wrote that <a href=\"https://keningzhu.com/journal/systems-as-sci-fi-bodysuits\" target=\"_blank\">systems are like sci-fi body suits</a>; that is, melded to the specific shape and idiosyncrasies of your creative body. any system you design <em>(processes, containers, plans, tools, rituals, routines, practices)</em> need to be made-to-fit YOUR specific body shapes, needs, and metabolisms. this means that instead of imposing a new productivity method on your psychic body (and then blaming your “lack of focus/discipline” when you abandon it after 5 days), you start, instead, with a gentle process of self-discovery. </p><p></p><h1>why make ongoing field notes </h1><p>so, instead of trying to make yourself <a href=\"https://keningzhu.com/journal/creative-systems-zine\" target=\"_blank\">function like a machine</a>, you treat yourself like a new, mysterious planet that you’re exploring, very slowly. </p><p>you seek to understand its climate, ecosystems, plant life, and rhythms — before you make it <em>do</em> anything for you. it’s about inhabitation, not exploitation. then, slowly, over time, you design systems that work <em>with</em> your inner urges, natural tendencies, and latent powers.  </p><p>I made this microbook as a tiny creative container; to write down, in the span of an hour or so, a distilled summary of what I know about how my system works. I didn’t overthink. I just sat down with 1 sheet of paper and a pen, and let it come out of me. instead of trying to come up with the “perfect, complete, definitive” field guide or plan, this is a practice about <a href=\"https://keningzhu.com/tiny-circles-as-completion-fuel\">the completion fuel of making one tiny circle</a>, which I wrote about last week. </p><p>I made this field guide over a month ago, and I can feel that I’m already in a different part of the spiraling journey. everything I wrote feels true — and yet, I also feel like it’s just one layer of the onion that I’m unpeeling. I expect to make a new field guide every time I feel ready to shed another layer. </p><p>this is part of the purpose, because truth is a glittering glacier, and we can never “know” every part of ourselves. beholding the mystery and being in reverence to it — is the point. </p><p></p><h1>prompts for making your own </h1><p>the simplest structure of this microbook is that of a “8 page zine fold” — created through a single A4 or 8.5x11 size piece of paper — though you can use any fold you want, with any number of pages you want. </p><p>here are the questions I’ve explored in my field guide. <br /><strong><br />what do I most VALUE? <br />what do I deeply DESIRE? <br />what’s my VISION? <br />what does my system NEED/REQUIRE to feel good? <br />what’s UNIQUE/SPECIFIC about my system, and how it works? <br /><em>when</em> does my system feel in FLOW? <br /><em>what</em> does it feel BLOCKED? <br />what PERMISSION do I give myself? <br />what’s my guiding COMPASS? </strong></p><p></p><p>if this sounds daunting, I encourage you to start with 1-2 key phrases or words — like grounding anchors, and let the rest come to you. jot down your notes and observations in the tiny, limited space that you have — without needing it to be perfect. complete it first. then come back to it. </p><p>if you’d like more guidance on inhabiting your creative ecosystem/planet, my <a href=\"https://keningzhu.com/creative-systems\">creative systems</a> self-paced course is my guided, living encapsulation of everything I have to offer, a potion I’ve simmered, to its most potent form. 